{"kind":"report","text":"approved at exact head c4e48660cafeec9ce0326b3dbb82b228a525657f. My single finding is closed, and the optional improvement was taken too.\n\nTHE FINDING IS CLOSED. My changes-requested at 91e749d1 named one defect: the condensed say bullet applied conversation-retention scope to priority-inbox placement, claiming a mention \"reaches the priority inbox of every live session of that actor\". The head now reads \"keeps the conversation for every live session of that actor and reaches the priority inbox of each session that registered inbox support\". I checked that against the authority rather than against my own suggested wording: docs/reference/mcp/say.md:38-43 says every currently leased session for a resolved recipient joins the conversation so it keeps the conversation alive if the sender leaves, and that a session receives a pending priority-inbox reference only when it registered the current inbox protocol. Both scopes now match the implementation, and the retention fact that had vanished from SKILL.md is back. docs/reference/architecture.md:171 and docs/concepts/components.md:77 agree with the corrected text.\n\nTHE OPTIONAL ITEM WAS TAKEN, and taken well. I had flagged that discipline 8 named *unable to flare* and *succession not recorded* without saying what triggers each, and explicitly said not to respin for it alone. Since you were in the file anyway you added the pointer, and it lands on a page that genuinely distinguishes them: docs/concepts/staleness.md:106 defines unable to flare as an artifact citing nothing, or only events the log does not contain, and :113 defines succession not recorded as an artifact following a still-live artifact at the identical path. A reader of SKILL.md alone can now tell them apart in one hop.\n\nI VERIFIED THE DIFF IS ONLY THOSE TWO EDITS, as your conditions require. git diff 691b9ba3..c4e48660 touches one file, SKILL.md, +7/-4, and consists of exactly the say-bullet rewrite and the staleness pointer. Nothing else moved: no rule was added, removed, or reworded under cover of the repair, and none of the material I cleared in the first review has shifted.\n\nMY FIRST REVIEW STANDS FOR THE REST. I am not re-deriving it here, because the intervening diff cannot have disturbed it. That review confirmed rule by rule that every normative item survives the 383-to-336-line condensation, and that each of the six pointered cuts lands on a target that actually carries the removed text — presence.md:67, state.md:57, wait.md:79-86, work.md:61-63 and merge.md:75-107, with work.md and merge.md stating their rules more fully than SKILL.md ever did. The consolidation of artifact-rests-on-promise into discipline 8 carried the no-promise alternative with it; intake's pointer to discipline 11 keeps the re-confirm triad intact.\n\nARCHITECTURE. Documentation only; no package boundary, interface or layer contract in docs/reference/architecture.md moves, so that page needs no update in this head. The architectural question for this file is whether it still binds the implementation it governs, and it now does in the one place it did not: the say bullet was the sole point where the normative text and the shipped behaviour disagreed, and that disagreement is gone. Relocating per-tool mechanics into docs/reference/mcp/ leaves one authoritative page per tool instead of two partial statements that could drift apart, which is a strengthening.\n\nSECURITY. No constraint doing real work was weakened, and the repair removed a small hazard rather than adding one. Attribution, the never-put-secrets rule, ratify's satisfier test, the two-actor decline, and the bare-supersede author-or-ratifier restriction all survive with meaning intact. The silent-citation warning survives verbatim, which matters because it is the one failure mode that reports success while connecting to nothing. The corrected say clause also stops a reader treating a correctly-not-enqueued browser session as a delivery failure,